# November 20, 2026: a first scatter of places across North Carolina, which had 13 (Old Salem and Winston-Salem, and
# Brunswick Town). From the Smokies to the Outer Banks: Mingus Mill and the Caldwell house at Cataloochee in the national
# park, Henry River Mill Village, Reed Gold Mine and Gold Hill in the gold belt, Yates Mill near Raleigh, Stagville near
# Durham, Edenton's 1767 courthouse, St. Thomas Church at Bath (begun 1734), Somerset Place and Hope Plantation, Fort
# Macon, Portsmouth Village, Bodie Island Lighthouse and Fort Fisher.
# Facts come from the linked sources only, read in this session: Wikipedia through tools/wiki_raw.py, and the managing
# bodies' own pages (NC Historic Sites, NC State Parks, the National Park Service, Wake County, the Historic Hope
# Foundation, the Historic Gold Hill page, St. Thomas parish, Henry River Mill Village) through tools/page.py. Hours are
# as those pages gave them on November 20, 2026. Where sources disagree the text gives both.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
BATCH={'key':'nc_qvkt','label':'North Carolina: a first scatter from the Smokies to the Outer Banks','added':'2026-11-20 04:25'}
NCHS='https://historicsites.nc.gov/all-sites/'
STATEHRS='Tuesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed Sunday, Monday and state holidays'
PLACES=[
N('mingus-mill-nc','Mingus Mill','Cherokee','nc','industry','standing',35.52011,-83.3098,'exact','public','walk','','1886',
  'A gristmill built in 1886 on Mingus Creek, half a mile north of what is now the Oconaluftee Visitor Center in Great Smoky Mountains National Park. It still stands where it was built. In place of a water wheel it runs all its machinery from a water-powered turbine.',
  'The mill building on its original site, with its machinery inside. The park says the interior is closed for now for preservation and rehabilitation work, but you can walk round the outside. In summer and early fall rangers have given milling programs there.',
  'A Smokies gristmill that never had a water wheel.',
  'Great Smoky Mountains National Park (National Park Service). Park at the end of Mingus Mill Access Road and walk across the bridge past the restrooms, as the park page directs. The park itself is open all day, every day; the mill interior was closed when the page was read (it was last updated October 2025).',
  [L('NPS: Mingus Mill','https://www.nps.gov/places/mingus-mill.htm'),L('NPS: Great Smoky Mountains operating hours','https://www.nps.gov/grsm/planyourvisit/hours.htm')],
  brick='?',hours='?',th=[35.52096,-83.30904],thn='Mingus Mill parking'),
N('caldwell-house-cataloochee','Caldwell house, Cataloochee','Cataloochee','nc','landmark','standing',35.62102,-83.11645,'exact','public','drive','','1903–1906',
  'Cataloochee was the largest community in the Smokies, about 1,251 people in Big and Little Cataloochee by 1910, and most of it emptied when the national park was made; few were left by 1938. Hiram Caldwell built this nine-room frame house between 1903 and 1906, one of the largest homes the park preserves. A barn and a springhouse go with it.',
  'The house and the barn are open and completely empty. Newspapers used as wallpaper still show lines of old articles, and there is elaborate woodwork inside. Elsewhere in Big Cataloochee stand Palmer Chapel, the Beech Grove School and other frame houses; the Palmer House has the Cataloochee history exhibits. Little Cataloochee is reached only on foot, a little over 2 miles on the Little Cataloochee Trail. Elk graze the mown fields.',
  'A mountain valley the park kept, and the newspaper wallpaper inside one of its houses.',
  'Great Smoky Mountains National Park. Drive to Cataloochee on the east side of the park; the house is about 2 miles past Cataloochee Campground, with parking across the road. A park marker says most sites in Big Cataloochee are reached by paved or gravel roads. Road conditions and seasonal closures were not checked.',
  [L('NPS: Caldwell Place','https://www.nps.gov/places/caldwell-place.htm'),L('NPS: History of Cataloochee','https://www.nps.gov/grsm/learn/historyculture/cataloochee-history.htm'),L('HMdb: Cataloochee (NPS marker)','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=230812')],
  brick='no'),
N('henry-river-mill-village','Henry River Mill Village','Hildebran','nc','town','standing',35.69566,-81.42919,'site','open','walk','','1904–1971',
  'Michael Erastus Rudisill laid out the mill and its village in 1904 on a 1,500-acre tract along the Henry River. From 1905 a 30-foot concrete dam and a three-story brick mill were built; the mill made fine cotton yarn, went from water to steam in 1914 and to electricity in 1926, and closed in 1971 (Wikipedia also says it shut in the late 1960s). The mill burned in 1977, probably from lightning. The village stood in the 2012 film The Hunger Games as District 12, was listed on the National Register in 2019, and since 2017 its owners have been restoring it for visitors.',
  'Mill houses along the steep north bank of the gorge (twenty of thirty-five were left, by Wikipedia), fieldstone retaining walls, and the two-story brick company store, which also served as the mill office and, upstairs, a school and church room from 1907 to 1917. The mill itself is gone. Some houses are let to overnight guests.',
  'District 12 in the films: a mill village that lost its mill.',
  'Private, run as a visitor attraction (4255 Henry River Road, Hickory; I-40 exit 119). General admission $18 for adults, children free with an adult, tickets online or on site; leashed pets welcome. Hours from the village\'s own page.',
  [W('Henry River Mill Village, North Carolina'),L('Henry River Mill Village: visitor information','https://henryrivermillvillage.com')],
  brick='?',hours='Winter: Saturday and Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; from March 20, 2026: Friday to Monday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('reed-gold-mine','Reed Gold Mine','Midland','nc','industry','standing',35.28553,-80.4665,'site','open','walk','','1799–1912',
  'In 1799 Conrad Reed found a 17-pound yellow rock in Little Meadow Creek on his father\'s farm; it served as a doorstop until a Fayetteville jeweler identified it as gold in 1802. This was the first documented gold find in the United States. John Reed began mining about 1803, went underground in 1831, and the last underground mining here was in 1912. North Carolina led the nation in gold until 1848.',
  'Several hundred feet of restored underground tunnels, shown by guided tour only, a museum of North Carolina gold mining, and gold panning in season.',
  'A doorstop that turned out to be America\'s first gold.',
  'Reed Gold Mine State Historic Site (NC Department of Natural and Cultural Resources), 9621 Reed Mine Road, Midland. Admission free; mine tours $5 for adults (10, 11, 12, 1:30, 2:30 and 3:30 when read), panning $5. Hours from the NC Historic Sites page.',
  [W('Reed Gold Mine'),L('NC Historic Sites: Reed Gold Mine',NCHS+'reed-gold-mine')],
  brick='?',hours=STATEHRS),
N('gold-hill-mines-park','Gold Hill Mines Historic Park','Gold Hill','nc','industry','ruins',35.51035,-80.3458,'site','public','walk','','1824–',
  'Gold was found here in 1824. The Barnhardt shaft, opened in 1842, reached 435 feet, and the Randolph (Earnhardt) shaft across the street, opened in 1843, reached 850 feet, by Wikipedia the deepest gold mine in the United States then. Gold Hill was incorporated in 1843 and had 3,000 people and 23 mines. George Barnhardt had learned mining at John Reed\'s mine. The park was begun in 1993 on donated land.',
  'In the park: a 19th-century Chilean ore mill, which the Gold Hill foundation thinks is the only one left in the country with all its original gears, a powder house built into the hillside in 1845, and a log barn of about 150 years at the entrance; a marker on the rail trail tells the Barnhardt story. In the village, the Montgomery General Store, Mauney\'s Store, the jail and a rock wall date from the 1840s (other buildings were moved in).',
  'The gold camp that Charlotte once envied.',
  'Gold Hill Mines Historic Park, beside the village shops; for GPS use 770 St. Stephens Church Road. No admission or parking fee; leashed pets welcome; no parking on the grass. The Gold Hill Rail Trail starts here. Village shops mostly open Friday to Sunday. Hours from the Historic Gold Hill page.',
  [W('Gold Hill, North Carolina'),L('Historic Gold Hill: the park and village','https://historicgoldhill.org/?p=50'),L('HMdb: Barnhardt marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=299634')],
  brick='?',hours='Park daily 8 a.m. to 8 p.m. while clocks are forward, 8 a.m. to 6 p.m. in winter'),
N('yates-mill-raleigh','Yates Mill','Raleigh','nc','industry','standing',35.71818,-78.6863,'exact','open','walk','','about 1756–1953',
  'The land was surveyed for Samuel Pearson in 1756 and the first mill built about then (Wake County\'s page says about 1760). Over nearly 200 years it ground corn and wheat, sawed lumber and carded wool; William Boylan added a sawmill in the 1840s, and Phares and Roxanna Yates bought it during the Civil War. Soldiers once tried to burn it. It closed in 1953, and Hurricane Fran burst its old stone dam in 1996; Yates Mill Associates and Wake County restored the mill, which opened to the public in 2006.',
  'The restored mill, of the Oliver Evans type, with its original corn and wheat machinery, and charred beams from the attempt to burn it. A visitor center, a 20-acre millpond and trails make up the 574-acre county park.',
  'The last working gristmill round Raleigh, with scorch marks to show.',
  'Historic Yates Mill County Park (Wake County Parks, Recreation and Open Space), run with the nonprofit Yates Mill Associates. Mill tours are free; Wake County gives the tour season as March to November, weekends. Park hours were not read.',
  [W('Yates Mill'),L('Wake County: Historic Yates Mill tours','https://www.wake.gov/departments-government/parks-recreation-open-space/all-parks-trails/historic-yates-mill-county-park/historic-yates-mill-programs-events/historic-yates-mill-historic-mill-tours')],
  th=[35.72011,-78.68725],thn='Yates Mill park lot (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?',hours='Mill tours March to November, weekends; in November 2026 open house Saturday and Sunday 1 to 4 p.m.'),
N('stagville-bennehan-house','Historic Stagville: Bennehan House','Durham','nc','landmark','standing',36.11999,-78.83669,'site','open','walk','','1787–1865',
  'Stagville was part of one of the largest plantation complexes in the South: about 30,000 acres owned by the Bennehan and Cameron families, who enslaved almost 900 people in 1860. The Bennehan House was built in 1787 with a large addition in 1799, by Wikipedia (the state gives it as about 1799). The state site now keeps 165 acres and tells the history of slavery here, working with descendants.',
  'The Bennehan family house; the Horton Grove quarters and a barn of 1860 are on the site\'s other tract. Guided tours go inside; the outdoor self-guided tour is free. Interior fabric not checked.',
  'One of the South\'s largest plantations, told from the quarters as well as the house.',
  'Historic Stagville State Historic Site, 5828 Old Oxford Highway, Durham. Admission free; guided tours $2, at 1 and 3 on weekdays and 11, 1 and 3 on Saturdays. Hours from the NC Historic Sites page.',
  [W('Stagville'),L('NC Historic Sites: Historic Stagville',NCHS+'historic-stagville')],
  brick='?',hours=STATEHRS),
N('horton-grove-stagville','Horton Grove slave quarters, Stagville','Durham','nc','landmark','standing',36.12498,-78.83997,'site','open','walk','','about 1850',
  'Two-story houses for enslaved people at Stagville, built in 1850 by Wikipedia (about 1851 by the state). They are well preserved and are the only two-story slave quarters left in North Carolina. Archaeology round them has told historians much about the lives of the people who lived and worked here.',
  'The two-story quarters of Horton Grove, on their own tract of the state site. Interior not checked.',
  'The only two-story slave quarters left in the state.',
  'Historic Stagville State Historic Site (see the Bennehan House entry): admission and the outdoor self-guided tour are free, guided tours $2. Pin from OpenStreetMap\'s Horton Grove point on Jock Road. Hours from the NC Historic Sites page.',
  [W('Stagville'),L('NC Historic Sites: Historic Stagville',NCHS+'historic-stagville')],
  brick='?',hours=STATEHRS),
N('chowan-county-courthouse','Chowan County Courthouse','Edenton','nc','civic','standing',36.05788,-76.60779,'site','open','walk','','1767',
  'Built in 1766 and 1767 and counted one of the finest public Georgian buildings in the South, a National Historic Landmark since 1970. Its design has been put down to Gilbert Leigh, who lived in Edenton then. Edenton\'s first courthouse, of 1719, stood on the green in front and also housed the colonial assembly.',
  'A two-story T-shaped brick building in Flemish bond with a semicircular apse at the back and a two-stage wooden cupola with a tall weathervane. Inside, a large courtroom on the ground floor and a ballroom upstairs; Wikipedia says it has seen only modest changes. A one-block park separates it from the waterfront.',
  'A 1767 courthouse with a ballroom upstairs.',
  'One of the sites of Historic Edenton State Historic Site, whose visitor center is at 108 N. Broad Street. Guided tour fees $5 for adults. Whether every tour goes into the courthouse was not checked; ask at the visitor center. Hours from the NC Historic Sites page.',
  [W('Chowan County Courthouse'),L('NC Historic Sites: Historic Edenton',NCHS+'historic-edenton')],
  brick='yes',hours='Visitor center '+STATEHRS+'; guided tours 10, 11, 1, 2 and 3 (summer hours)'),
N('st-thomas-church-bath','St. Thomas Episcopal Church','Bath','nc','worship','standing',35.47414,-76.81288,'exact','open','walk','','1734–1762',
  'Begun in 1734 under the Rev. John Garzia and finished in 1762, it is the oldest surviving church building in North Carolina and has been in use ever since. Bath, founded in 1705, was the colony\'s first town. The parish had the colony\'s first public library, about 1,050 books sent by the Rev. Thomas Bray around 1700.',
  'Brick walls two feet thick laid in Flemish bond, and square red paving tiles with flowers and dragons, by the state\'s history page. The church keeps Queen Anne\'s Bell (the same page says it was cast in 1732 in one place and in 1750, recast 1872, in another), a 1765 slate memorial to Margaret Palmer, a silver chalice from the Bishop of London (1738) and silver candelabra of about 1740.',
  'The oldest church in North Carolina, still holding Sunday services.',
  'An active Episcopal parish, 101 Craven Street, in the Historic Bath district (the state historic site\'s visitor center is at 100 S. Harding Street). The parish page welcomes visitors passing through and holds services in the old church every Sunday. Service times are from the parish page for Sunday, November 22, 2026.',
  [W('St. Thomas Episcopal Church (Bath, North Carolina)'),L('St. Thomas Episcopal Church (parish page)','https://www.stthomasparishnc.org'),L('NC Historic Sites: St. Thomas Episcopal Church',NCHS+'historic-bath/history/st-thomas-episcopal-church')],
  brick='?',hours='Sunday Holy Eucharist 8 and 10 a.m.'),
N('somerset-place','Somerset Place','Creswell','nc','landmark','standing',35.78848,-76.40406,'site','open','walk','','1785–1865',
  'A plantation on the north shore of Lake Phelps from 1785 to 1865, carved from more than 100,000 acres of swampy woods; by 1865 it was one of the largest in the Upper South, with rice, grain and flax fields and sawmills. It became a State Historic Site in 1969. In 1986 descendants of the people enslaved here held the first Somerset Homecoming.',
  'The 1830s plantation house, the dairy, the kitchen and laundry, a smokehouse and a salting house, with reconstructed quarters and plantation hospital. The Collins House was closed for a construction project when the state page was read; the rest of the site stays open. Trails lead into Pettigrew State Park next door.',
  'A great swamp plantation, and the homecoming its descendants began.',
  'Somerset Place State Historic Site, 2572 Lake Shore Road, Creswell. Admission and self-guided tours free; guided tours $5 for adults. Hours from the NC Historic Sites page.',
  [W('Somerset Place'),L('NC Historic Sites: Somerset Place',NCHS+'somerset-place')],
  brick='?',hours=STATEHRS),
N('hope-plantation','Hope Plantation','Windsor','nc','landmark','standing',36.03196,-77.01768,'area','open','walk','','1763–1803',
  'David Stone, later governor of North Carolina and a U.S. senator, built the house about 1803, a Palladian house of the Federal style, rare for being built of timber. After nearly a century of neglect it was bought and restored by the Historic Hope Foundation and opened in 1972. The King-Bazemore House of 1763, a hall-and-parlor house, stands on the same grounds.',
  'The mansion\'s five-bay front with a pedimented double portico and its original Chinese Chippendale balustrade, a widow\'s walk on the roof, and tall rooms furnished with period pieces; the 1763 King-Bazemore House is furnished from its owner\'s 1778 inventory. Guided tours take in both houses and the gardens.',
  'A governor\'s Palladian house, built in wood.',
  'Historic Hope Foundation, 132 Hope House Road, four miles west of Windsor. The Heritage Center museum and grounds are free; the houses by guided tour (ticket prices on the foundation\'s page). The foundation\'s page did not give hours when read.',
  [W('Hope Plantation'),L('Historic Hope Foundation','https://hopeplantation.org'),L('Historic Hope: visit','https://hopeplantation.org/visit/')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('fort-macon','Fort Macon','Atlantic Beach','nc','fort','standing',34.69624,-76.6791,'exact','open','walk','forts','1826–1903',
  'A five-sided fort of the Third System, begun in 1826 and finished in December 1834 to guard Beaufort Inlet; the earlier Fort Hampton had been lost to the shifting inlet. Confederates held it until the siege of March and April 1862, and the Union kept it for the rest of the war. The Army left in 1903, and the Civilian Conservation Corps restored it in 1934 and 1935 for North Carolina\'s first state park, opened in 1936.',
  'The restored brick and stone fort: 26 vaulted casemates inside outer walls 4.5 feet thick. Daily guided tours, and cannon and musket demonstrations.',
  'A brick fort the CCC restored for the state\'s first park.',
  'Fort Macon State Park (NC Division of Parks and Recreation), on Bogue Banks east of Atlantic Beach. No fee for day use; pets on a 6-foot leash, not inside the fort. Closed Christmas Day. The fort parking area keeps its own seasonal hours (8 a.m. to 6 to 8 p.m.). Hours from the park\'s page.',
  [W('Fort Macon State Park'),L('NC State Parks: Fort Macon','https://www.ncparks.gov/state-parks/fort-macon-state-park')],
  th=[34.69795,-76.67834],thn='Fort parking area',brick='some',hours='Fort and visitor center daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed Christmas Day'),
N('portsmouth-village-nc','Portsmouth Village','Portsmouth Island','nc','town','standing',35.06855,-76.05758,'site','boat','boat','','1753–1971',
  'Founded by the colonial assembly in 1753 as a lightering port on Ocracoke Inlet, where cargo was moved from ocean ships to boats shallow enough for the sounds. It peaked at 685 people in 1860. Hurricanes in 1846 opened better inlets, the harbor shoaled and the Civil War emptied it further. The last two residents left in 1971, after the death of Henry Pigott; the village is now part of Cape Lookout National Seashore.',
  'The Salter House (the visitor center), the one-room school, the Methodist church, the Life-Saving Station of 1894, Henry Pigott\'s house and the post office and general store, open to the public in summer by Wikipedia. Very few facilities: a compost toilet by the Life-Saving Station, a restroom at the visitor center, no drinking water, food or power.',
  'A port town abandoned in 1971, reached only by ferry.',
  'Cape Lookout National Seashore (National Park Service). By passenger ferry from Ocracoke village, or with a four-wheel-drive vehicle by the vehicle ferry from Atlantic and the beach tracks of North Core Banks. Camping is not allowed in the village. The visitor center, in the first house on the right of the path from Haulover Dock, is open seasonally; the islands are open around the clock, weather permitting.',
  [W('Portsmouth, North Carolina'),L('NPS: Cape Lookout visitor centers','https://home.nps.gov/calo/planyourvisit/visitorcenters.htm'),L('NPS: Cape Lookout operating hours','https://www.nps.gov/calo/planyourvisit/hours.htm')],
  brick='?',hours='Visitor center seasonal, Monday to Friday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('bodie-island-lighthouse','Bodie Island Lighthouse','Nags Head','nc','light','standing',35.8185,-75.5633,'exact','open','drive','','1872',
  'The third lighthouse of the name, finished in 1872 and 156 feet tall, on the Roanoke Sound side just south of Nags Head. The first, of 1847, stood south of Oregon Inlet on ground now under water and was abandoned in 1859; the second, of 1859, was blown up by retreating Confederates in 1861. Restored from 2009 to 2013, the tower was then opened to climbers.',
  'One of about a dozen tall brick lighthouse towers left in the United States, with its original first-order Fresnel lens, and just over 200 steps (Wikipedia says 219) to the top.',
  'A climbable 1872 tower with its first-order lens still in it.',
  'Cape Hatteras National Seashore (National Park Service). Climbing tickets $10 for adults and $5 for seniors, children 11 and under and the disabled, sold the same day only from 7 a.m. on recreation.gov; climbers must be at least 42 inches tall. Hours from the park\'s lighthouse-climbs page.',
  [W('Bodie Island Lighthouse'),L('NPS: Lighthouse climbs','https://www.nps.gov/caha/planyourvisit/lighthouseclimbs.htm')],
  brick='no',hours='Climbing late April to early November 2026; October to early November only Tuesday to Thursday'),
N('fort-fisher-nc','Fort Fisher','Kure Beach','nc','fort','traces',33.9717,-77.9194,'site','open','walk','forts','1861–1865',
  'A Confederate earthwork fort that kept the port of Wilmington open to blockade runners from 1861, its land face a line of fifteen sand mounds joined by passages, with the Mound Battery of 1863 at the sea end. A huge Union assault by land and sea took it on January 15, 1865, the most decisive battle of the war in North Carolina. Sea erosion, the building of US 421 and a World War II airstrip destroyed most of the mounds.',
  'A few surviving sand mounds of the land face, with a tour trail and markers round them; part of the palisade fence has been rebuilt. A museum and visitor center are on the site.',
  'The "Gibraltar of the South", mostly washed away.',
  'Fort Fisher State Historic Site, 1610 Fort Fisher Boulevard South, Kure Beach. Admission free. Hours from the NC Historic Sites page.',
  [W('Fort Fisher'),L('NC Historic Sites: Fort Fisher',NCHS+'fort-fisher')],
  th=[33.97246,-77.91693],thn='Fort Fisher site lot (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',hours=STATEHRS),
]
